        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "dcc67488-08ad-57e5-9783-3ba6f91c2734",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Frances Sweeney, Charles Coughlin\n- Organizations: National Union for Social Justice\n- Places: Boston, Detroit, Royal Oak, Michigan\n- Roles: journalist, activist, Roman Catholic priest, radio host\n- Products: Boston City Reporter, Boston Herald Rumor Clinic\n- Topics: fascism, antisemitism, political corruption, disinformation\n\nFacts:\n- Frances Sweeney was born around 1908 and died on June 19, 1944.\n- In 1934, Charles Coughlin formed the National Union for Social Justice.\n- Charles Coughlin was a Canadian-American Roman Catholic priest and radio host.\n- Charles Coughlin was based in the United States near Detroit at the National Shrine of the Little Flower church in Royal Oak, Michigan.\n- Frances Sweeney was a journalist and activist who campaigned against fascism, antisemitism, and political corruption in 1940s Boston.\n- Frances Sweeney edited her own newspaper, the Boston City Reporter.\n- Frances Sweeney started the Boston Herald Rumor Clinic to combat fascist disinformation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "3fa0a25b-6eec-532b-9ea5-70c59b339bf4",
        "text": "Page: Frances Sweeney. Sentence: Frances Sweeney (c. 1908 – June 19, 1944) was a journalist and activist who campaigned against fascism, antisemitism, and political corruption in 1940s Boston.\nPage: Frances Sweeney. Sentence: She edited her own newspaper, the \"Boston City Reporter\", and started the \"Boston Herald\" Rumor Clinic to combat fascist disinformation.\nPage: Charles Coughlin. Sentence: Charles Edward Coughlin ( ; October 25, 1891 – October 27, 1979), was a controversial Canadian-American Roman Catholic priest based in the United States near Detroit at Royal Oak, Michigan's National Shrine of the Little Flower church.\nPage: National Union for Social Justice (organization). Sentence: The National Union for Social Justice was a political movement formed in 1934 by Rev. Charles Coughlin, a Roman Catholic priest and radio host.",
